messages:
Maria: fyi getting a steady stream of CF Workers bug reports for the JS/TS SDK. Symptoms: 'require is not defined', references to process/fs/Buffer, and weird typing errors in TS.
Kevin: are these user bundles or our published package? CF Workers expects ESM.
Maria: user repro attached: they used CJS require in a worker: `const { RedwoodClient } = require('redwood');` -> deploy fails.
Sam: Workers are ESM-first. Action: publish an ESM entrypoint, add conditional exports in package.json, and provide a worker-specific bundle that avoids Node-only libs.
Olivia: CF Workers have no Node builtins (no process, fs, Buffer). Their globals (fetch, TextEncoder, ReadableStream) exist but differ subtly. We must avoid bundling node-fetch/stream shims into worker builds.
Maria: sample problematic snippet the user pasted:
```
// index.js (user)
const { RedwoodClient } = require('redwood');
addEventListener('fetch', e => e.respondWith(handle(e.request)));
async function handle(req) {
  const c = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: __ENV.REDWOOD_KEY });
  const r = await c.generate('hello');
  return new Response(r.text);
}
```
Error: "require is not defined" on deploy.
Kevin: two-prong fix: 1) publish dist/redwood.esm.mjs as import entrypoint; 2) add an exports map that points `import` -> esm, `require` -> cjs, and a worker/browser branch -> dist/redwood.worker.mjs so bundlers pick the right file.
Sam: suggested exports snippet (conceptual):
```json
"exports": {
  "import": "./dist/redwood.esm.mjs",
  "require": "./dist/redwood.cjs.js",
  "worker": "./dist/redwood.worker.mjs",
  "browser": {
    "import": "./dist/redwood.browser.mjs"
  }
}
```
Olivia: worker build requirements: remove any modules that reference process.env at runtime, replace Buffer with Uint8Array, and don't expose Node Stream types in public surface. Also avoid optionalDeps that pull polyfills.
Maria: auth/retry layer also tripped users. We bundle node-fetch for retry/backoff; in Workers there is global fetch and AbortSignal semantics differ.
Kevin: make fetch pluggable. API pattern: `new RedwoodClient({ fetch: globalThis.fetch, apiKey: env.REDWOOD_KEY })` and default to node-fetch only when globalThis.fetch is missing. Add a tiny fetch-adapter module.
Sam: agree. Also watch TextEncoder/TextDecoder, ReadableStream differences. Return strings or Uint8Array instead of Node Buffer.
Olivia: typing fix: our d.ts references NodeJS.ReadableStream and imports Node types. Edge users hit type conflicts. Publish a `types/worker.d.ts` (small ambient declarations) and include it in the worker conditional export. Example: `declare interface ReadableStreamLike { getReader(): any }`.
Maria: detection helper for docs we used in repros:
```ts
// detect-edge.ts
export const isEdge = typeof globalThis?.fetch === 'function' && typeof process === 'undefined';
```
usage: `const fetchImpl = opts.fetch ?? (isEdge ? globalThis.fetch : nodeFetch);`
Kevin: add CI smoke tests using miniflare/wrangler so we run simple SDK flows in a worker-like runtime. That will catch Node-only regressions slipping in.
Sam: I'll add an example under examples/worker/ with esbuild config: `{ format: 'esm', target: 'es2020', platform: 'browser' }` and the quickstart:
```js
import { RedwoodClient } from 'redwood';
export default {
  async fetch(request, env) {
    const c = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: env.REDWOOD_KEY, fetch: globalThis.fetch });
    const r = await c.generate('hi');
    return new Response(r.text);
  }
}
```
Maria: docs should call out pitfalls: no top-level form-data/node-fetch, avoid dynamic require(), don't assume Buffer, and prefer passing env.apiKey into constructor.
Olivia: I'll prepare types patches to avoid Node references and add `types/worker.d.ts`. Also add a short README section on passing `fetch` and env into the client.
Kevin: I'll open the exports/build PR. Sam will add example + CI miniflare job. Olivia will update types. Maria will draft support doc and quickstart.
deploy-bot: pipeline started for branch fix/edge-shim by sam  worker tests running.
Maria: thanks team  once PRs land we'll update support ticket replies and publish migration notes. :rocket:
